(pub :: sysRenameNum Int)

(pub fn (sysRenameNum) 33554560)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Reading a directory.
;
; `getdirentries64(fd, buf, bufsize, off_t *position)` - BSD 344, and
; NOT `getdirentries` (BSD 196), which carries the 32-bit-inode
; `struct dirent` that APFS cannot express.
;
; The fourth argument is an in/out cookie the kernel keeps the scan
; position in, and it has no Linux counterpart - `getdents64` is three
; arguments and keeps the position on the descriptor. That difference
; is what `dirReadNeedsPosition` selects, the same way
; `openNeedsDirFd` selects `open` from `openat`.
(pub :: sysGetdentsNum Int)

(pub fn (sysGetdentsNum) 33554776)

(pub :: dirReadNeedsPosition Int)

(pub fn (dirReadNeedsPosition) 1)

; Where the name starts inside one record. Darwin's 64-bit `dirent` is
;
;   u64 d_ino; u64 d_seekoff; u16 d_reclen; u16 d_namlen; u8 d_type;
;   char d_name[];
;
; so d_reclen sits at 16 - which Linux's `linux_dirent64` also does,
; and portable code relies on - and the name begins at 21 rather than
; Linux's 19, because of the d_namlen field Linux has no room for.
; Probed on this host 2026-08-15: `.` and `..` come back with
; d_reclen 32, d_namlen 1 and 2, d_type 4.
(pub :: direntNameOffset Int)

(pub fn (direntNameOffset) 21)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The working directory.
;
; Darwin's syscall table HAS NO `getcwd`. Number 326 was `__getcwd`
; historically and is a hole in the current table - probed on this
; host 2026-08-15, a program calling it dies with SIGSYS - so the
; portable spelling cannot be "one number per platform".
;
; `fcntl(fd, F_GETPATH, buf)` is the way in, and the reason it is
; acceptable where `stat` is not (see `Sys.ax`) is that its answer is
; BYTES, not a record: a NUL-terminated path written into a
; caller-supplied buffer, with no offsets, widths or padding to get
; wrong. It needs a descriptor, so `sysGetCwd` opens `.` first.
;
; `sysCwdNum` is `fcntl` here and `getcwd` on Linux; the two have
; different shapes and `cwdUsesFcntlPath` picks between them.
(pub :: cwdUsesFcntlPath Int)

(pub fn (cwdUsesFcntlPath) 1)

; fcntl(fd, cmd, arg) - BSD 92.
(pub :: sysCwdNum Int)

(pub fn (sysCwdNum) 33554524)

(pub :: fGetPath Int)

(pub fn (fGetPath) 50)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The two errno values portable code above compares against.
;
; They live here because errno numbering is per-OS ABI and DOES
; diverge - EAGAIN is 35 on Darwin and 11 on Linux - so a portable
; `(== e 21)` is a coincidence waiting to be wrong on the next target
; rather than a fact. These two happen to agree across all four
; targets today, and naming them here is what keeps that a
; measurement instead of an assumption.
(pub :: eExist Int)

(pub fn (eExist) 17)

(pub :: eIsDir Int)

(pub fn (eIsDir) 21)

; getpid() - BSD 20
(pub :: sysGetPidNum Int)

(pub fn (sysGetPidNum) 33554452)

; gettimeofday(tv, tz) - BSD 116. Writes {tv_sec i64, tv_usec i32+pad}
; to its 16-byte buffer; the register-return interpretation was probed
; WRONG on arm64 (x0 is 0 on success, never seconds). Verified against
; the shell clock, 400,000 reads, zero backwards steps, ~200ns/call.
(pub :: sysClockNum Int)

(pub fn (sysClockNum) 33554548)

(pub :: clockIsGettimeofday Int)

(pub fn (clockIsGettimeofday) 1)

; Whether this platform can answer a MONOTONIC clock - one that never
; steps backwards - through a syscall. Darwin cannot, and that is the
; whole reason this capability exists rather than being assumed.
;
; `gettimeofday` above is REALTIME. It is the right clock for "what
; time is it" and the wrong one for every timeout, rate limiter and
; retry backoff, because `ntpd` may step it backwards at any moment and
; a duration computed across that step is negative. The comment above
; records 400,000 reads with zero backwards steps; that measured the
; absence of a step during the test, not the absence of the hazard.
;
; Darwin's BSD table has no `clock_gettime`: libSystem's is built over
; the commpage, not a syscall, so reaching it costs the freestanding
; property `check-freestanding.sh` exists to hold (0 undefined symbols).
;
; The fix that keeps that property is available and is NOT a syscall.
; On arm64 the monotonic counter and its frequency are readable from
; user space as system registers - `mrs x0, CNTVCT_EL0` and `mrs x0,
; CNTFRQ_EL0` - which is what `mach_absolute_time` reads underneath.
; That needs a codegen primitive to emit the `mrs`, and a primitive
; cannot be spelled here until the committed seed knows it, so it lands
; as a compiler change plus `scripts/reseed.sh` rather than as a line in
; this file. darwin-x86_64 is harder and separate: `rdtsc` needs the
; commpage's scaling factors and is not invariant on all hardware.
;
; Until then `sysNowMonotonic` answers -ENOSYS here rather than
; substituting the realtime clock, so a caller that needs a duration
; finds out instead of being handed one that can run backwards.
(pub :: clockHasMonotonic Int)

(pub fn (clockHasMonotonic) 0)
